PASS level_intents: all 66 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 6.8/10 (bar 8), 8 level(s) below 6/10: The core is strong and unusually honest. Every gem shows its real risk, each unit has a one-line "aha", and defence is a telegraphed read rather than twitch. Chapters 4–6 have good formation and rival themes, and 22, 33, 42, 55 and 61 are memorable. Five problems hold the set back. (1) Kick, ghost-score and "string touches then Dubki" units are re-skinned too often. Kick is taught or re-taught in 23, 27, 32, 43, 49, 57, 60 and 65. (2) The displayed % often makes one option strictly dominant, so there is no decision: bonus 4% in 18, Kick 3% in 23 and 27, Dubki in 14, 48 and 53. (3) Several units contradict their own intent or hide risk behind the READ row. Those are 9, 16, 24, 27, 60, 64 and 65. (4) Skill barely matters in some late units. In 36, 47 and 65 the competent and casual win rates differ by only 2–9 points, and best-star results are 2★ in 16, 43 and 47. (5) Meta pillars arrive late or never pay off. The auction first appears at unit 34. Auctions end in "buy X" with no visible squad payoff. DRS, Super Raid, lunge and the Kit Album/upgrades never return after their lesson. As shipped it is a very good tutorial-puzzle campaign, but it is not yet a league-career game of the EA FC Mobile / Top Eleven calibre. Curve: Difficulty falls steadily, as intended: casual win rate goes from about 90% in chapter 1 to about 62% in chapters 5–6. Several late units are flat or random, though. 47, 49, 60 and 65 have a casual/skilled gap of 2–9 points, so extra effort does not help the player. The 11-units-per-chapter template is rigid and predictable: teach, teach, twist, breather, spike or combine, then boss. The breather sits directly before the boss (21→22, 32→33, 43→44, 54→55) instead of after the hardest unit, so the rest comes before the test, not after the spike. Chapter 1 stays too easy for too long: 9 of 11 units have a casual win rate of 85% or more, and it contains a "spike" at 85%. Introductions are uneven. The Backward Kick is taught four times in chapter 3 and then used as a toy through chapter 6. The auction, the headline team-builder pillar, first appears at 34 of 66. Scouting appears untaught in 27, and the unit's own intent contradicts it. Super Raid, DRS, lunge and the bonus hop are each used once or twice and then dropped. The late chapters pile on constraints (grip 1.3–1.46, walk-home risk of 60–80%, scouting, feints, fatigue), so some units are won by variance. Retention hooks (ghost ladder, Kit Album, upgrades, rivals) are mostly absent from the content sequence. Only Sameer and Arun recur as rivals. Fix this with: (a) reward and unlock beats after every boss; (b) a squad-upgrade unit in chapter 3; (c) a cap of two Kick units; (d) a recurring named rival arc through chapters 3–6; (e) sequencing so rest follows the spike, not precedes the boss.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L47 (4/10): Casual 65% / skilled 67% makes it luck. Best result is 2★. A failed tackle costs two men and the Dubki ducks the chain. It repeats L26. → Merge with L26 and lower the opponent's land rate.
L16 (5/10): The intent says Toe counters T, but the READ shows T h35/t36 and A h21/t34, so the 'counter' is a lie. 3★ needs an empty raid and zero catches at 35% risk per touch. Best stars are 2★. → Re-tune the READ so counters show a 15-point gap, and drop the 'never get caught' star.
L24 (5/10): Pull and lunge both read 44% at the start, so the 'shallow → lunge' rule can't be seen on the gems. Held rates are 31–39% on every touch, which makes it an RNG gate. → Show the lunge % that updates with depth, and cut the grip on at least half the mat.
L26 (5/10): Starting with 3 on the mat, one two-touch raid (81% land) fails the unit instantly. Competent players win 34/40 and the unit punishes the exact greed it teaches. → Allow concede up to 3 or use a one-touch raider on raids 1–2.
L27 (5/10): A re-skin of L23 with more R's. The intent says 'no scouting' but the mat is SCOUTED. Scouting is never taught. → Cut or merge with L23; teach scouting in its own unit.
L43 (5/10): The fourth Kick-based score attack. Reach 7 is introduced without explanation. → Replace it with a squad-upgrade showcase or a Dubki/bonus trick-shot toy.
L50 (5/10): The first close call is rigged 'very tight' and wrong, so the player has no real choice, and the unit demands a win via the review. DRS never returns. → Use randomised calls with visible odds, and reuse DRS in a later boss.
L65 (5/10): The Kick on the captain reads 15% but lands only 2 in 3, a hidden risk that violates 'the number is your risk'. Casual 61% / skilled 70%. It stacks feints, scouting, a captain and Hard. → Show the combined fail % on the gem and drop one layer.
L6 (6/10): Almost the same drill as L4 (hand-only, thigh always right) with a ring overlay. The only decision is which neighbour to tap. → Combine with L4's mixed hand/toe so the ring changes the choice, or fold it into L4.
L9 (6/10): Intent says the reversal comes after 2 raids each, the data says after raid 4 of 4. If it happens after 4, the player gets no raid on the new mat. The reversal mat also differs from the intent. → Reverse at the midpoint (after raid 2 each) and re-sync the text and data.
L10 (6/10): 85% casual win, an Easy raider and 2 points in 8 raids are not a spike; it plays like another match. → Use a Medium raider and 12-15 start score with a clock, or shrink to 3 raids each.
L14 (6/10): Introduces Dubki, the wall and the Toe at once. Dubki 15% vs walk 46% and Dash 69% is a no-choice lesson. Mid-skill win rate is 78%. → Teach Dubki on a soft mat first, then bring in the wall.
L17 (6/10): Corner A Hand at 3% held makes Do-or-Die tension nil; the 'greedy' star is the only drama. → Make the best safe touch about 20–25% so Do-or-Die actually feels dangerous.
L18 (6/10): Bonus at 4% held is strictly dominant and free. 'Bonus first' is a rote order with no risk. → Give the bonus hop a real risk (15–25%) and put rookies only on one side.
L21 (6/10): Only a score target, with no interaction with a person. Bonus at 5% is free points, and 'beat 4 in 4 raids' is trivially hit. → Show the ghost's actual replay with a named friend and a head-to-head per-raid comparison.
L30 (6/10): Seven verbs and 22 options at start; 65% mid-skill for a combine at 4 raids is rough. Held rates of 35–47% make wins variance-driven. → Lower grip, or drop Dubki and the bonus from the first half.
L32 (6/10): Same structure as 21 with a Rusher added. A 3-point target is trivial. → Add the friend's name and a one-off ghost quirk (the ghost uses the bonus), and raise the target.
L35 (6/10): Specialists only sit on the corners, so they matter on a small share of raids, and the ×1.25 is hard to feel. → Put the specialists in the centre and make the multiplier visible as a gem delta.
L36 (6/10): Casual 75% vs skilled 79% shows skill barely matters. The rule set (±1 range, half strength outside) is dense. → Split into 'Anchor only' and 'corner vs Anchor' units.
L39 (6/10): The instant switch exists from L6; this is L6/L35 with a second touch. → Fold it into the L35 follow-up.
L44 (6/10): A 7-lot auction plus ~14 raids exceeds a session. The player's side shows 'o o o o o o o', so the auction has no visible effect. Mid-skill win rate is 72% on a grip 1.31 mat. → Split into auction and match, show the purchased squad on the mat, and cut to ~10 raids.
L48 (6/10): Dubki 19% vs walk/dash 60% is a solved single answer. Scouting is just a tax. → Add a second way to beat the Cage (e.g. a Toe on the chain links).
L49 (6/10): The displayed held rates are 3–14% but the mid-skill win is 68%, so hidden failure sources contradict the 'real risk' rule. → Surface the Kick/bonus execution risk in the READ.
L52 (6/10): Held rates of 48–53% on key slots with a 70% mid-skill; scouting plus 2 touches makes it a slog. → Lower grip to 0.85 and drop scouting.
L53 (6/10): Needs 5 of 5 with walk 75%; success relies on a single greedy raid. The ghost is a number, not an opponent. → Cut the target to 4, or make the ghost's trace visible.

level-design score 6.8/10 — The core is strong and unusually honest. Every gem shows its real risk, each unit has a one-line "aha", and defence is a telegraphed read rather than twitch. Chapters 4–6 have good formation and rival themes, and 22, 33, 42, 55 and 61 are memorable. Five problems hold the set back. (1) Kick, ghost-score and "string touches then Dubki" units are re-skinned too often. Kick is taught or re-taught in 23, 27, 32, 43, 49, 57, 60 and 65. (2) The displayed % often makes one option strictly dominant, so there is no decision: bonus 4% in 18, Kick 3% in 23 and 27, Dubki in 14, 48 and 53. (3) Several units contradict their own intent or hide risk behind the READ row. Those are 9, 16, 24, 27, 60, 64 and 65. (4) Skill barely matters in some late units. In 36, 47 and 65 the competent and casual win rates differ by only 2–9 points, and best-star results are 2★ in 16, 43 and 47. (5) Meta pillars arrive late or never pay off. The auction first appears at unit 34. Auctions end in "buy X" with no visible squad payoff. DRS, Super Raid, lunge and the Kit Album/upgrades never return after their lesson. As shipped it is a very good tutorial-puzzle campaign, but it is not yet a league-career game of the EA FC Mobile / Top Eleven calibre.
curve: Difficulty falls steadily, as intended: casual win rate goes from about 90% in chapter 1 to about 62% in chapters 5–6. Several late units are flat or random, though. 47, 49, 60 and 65 have a casual/skilled gap of 2–9 points, so extra effort does not help the player. The 11-units-per-chapter template is rigid and predictable: teach, teach, twist, breather, spike or combine, then boss. The breather sits directly before the boss (21→22, 32→33, 43→44, 54→55) instead of after the hardest unit, so the rest comes before the test, not after the spike. Chapter 1 stays too easy for too long: 9 of 11 units have a casual win rate of 85% or more, and it contains a "spike" at 85%. Introductions are uneven. The Backward Kick is taught four times in chapter 3 and then used as a toy through chapter 6. The auction, the headline team-builder pillar, first appears at 34 of 66. Scouting appears untaught in 27, and the unit's own intent contradicts it. Super Raid, DRS, lunge and the bonus hop are each used once or twice and then dropped. The late chapters pile on constraints (grip 1.3–1.46, walk-home risk of 60–80%, scouting, feints, fatigue), so some units are won by variance. Retention hooks (ghost ladder, Kit Album, upgrades, rivals) are mostly absent from the content sequence. Only Sameer and Arun recur as rivals. Fix this with: (a) reward and unlock beats after every boss; (b) a squad-upgrade unit in chapter 3; (c) a cap of two Kick units; (d) a recurring named rival arc through chapters 3–6; (e) sequencing so rest follows the spike, not precedes the boss.
